<chapter>

<title><classname>HttpdDirectory</classname> Reference</title>
 <section id="fsDirectory" xreflabel="directory"><title>Introduction</title>
  
  <para>
   For those filesystems which support the concept of hierarchical
   namespaces or file listings, 
   <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname> objects provide the ability 
   to traverse one directory's contents in a linear fashion.  
  </para>

 </section>

 <section><title>Public Methods</title>

  <section><title><function>Name</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       const char
       *<function><classname>HttpdDirectory</classname>::Name</function>
      </funcdef><void>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Return the currently loaded directory entry in this
    <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname> object.  The syntax
    of the resultant string is entirely system-dependent.
   </para>

   <para>
    The provided string pointer is valid until the originating
    <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname> is closed.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>Next</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       bool
       <function><classname>HttpdDirectory</classname>::Next</function>
      </funcdef><void>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Load the next directory entry in series within the parent
    <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname> object.   
   </para>

   <para>
    Returns <returnvalue>true</returnvalue> if successful,
    <returnvalue>false</returnvalue> if no further directory entries
    exist.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>Close</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       void
       <function><classname>HttpdDirectory</classname>::Close</function>
      </funcdef><void>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Destroy the <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname> object and release
    any allocated resources. After calling this method the pointer to
    the <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname> is no longer valid.
   </para>
  </section>

 </section>

 <section><title>Public Data</title>

  <para>
   <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname> contains no publically
   accessible data members.
  </para>

 </section>

</chapter>

